#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124339 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "dfb93cc8-6bf0-59d4-8fbc-c94f5c0c1a69" :question/math-academy-id "e-5502" :question/problem "Find $A^{100},$ where \n![](images/81/81d78f5e129614d85d3719539ab9afc1a04307afb3b03eb9d7a55829ee6354c9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that\n\n![](images/43/43438bad181467e28a2e0758f7b7aa070fd5f5de37e1a6e19d4cabb591dc4f7d.svg)\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/93/93e5e9f9947fb4299da165192f103d7be7494ecc752edacf1a4931d4766337ef.svg)\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "737021ad-5198-564f-bbe2-cc5a06ee5a82" :question/math-academy-id "e-5503" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n![](images/06/063c1f52097f096bc39a2f8ba29f251c655c926a542828dfba50bcd3bb29dcea.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/84/84fd9d7243958aa329a23c3c9f19e7c35b8969699511b72bdfc4546b4577fc4a.svg)\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/fd/fd405e80c68f63df281c9f3e2fe4dd8ab25e32a0214619a7e5220c7e01944ab8.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" :question/math-academy-id "e-7133" :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](images/e3/e38c742a22bea651a3c410744f798297306661b6eee2929d7c5efff3bdb78844.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](images/eb/ebedba78b53f92ed5e4d69322a5e682675bdfc13b2687575fdf94f9c78a1cbe5.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/16/16933b8fd024b62ad8fafa36b382aefce74383a9bf47b952bf7e0e12fe26cae6.svg) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270ce863-fbe7-5c33-a666-b3f67ca41c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487647} {:answer/id #uuid "49d9d9a6-36c5-5844-a7e2-9ab72fd10336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487710} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f3b8b-d239-5334-86f9-ac773c0104db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487886} {:answer/id #uuid "cac37090-eba4-5d8f-8620-ada8f0124892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487954} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fe7ace-fda9-51ce-8e39-10efff3a05df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270ce863-fbe7-5c33-a666-b3f67ca41c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487647} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3d1395-4451-583c-ab82-a35d131d2416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11fac3f6-18af-5205-bfbf-77c7cc7c000b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140129" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11613836-dbb4-5662-a981-48ed81805197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487598} {:answer/id #uuid "355d0cf5-0e21-5469-ba66-35bad7819086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487668} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6dd37f-b440-589a-b70e-3b2462fa691a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487722} {:answer/id #uuid "576e07b7-0fb2-5793-abac-ace0c9367092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487741} {:answer/id #uuid "b77c8c33-838e-5a30-8ab4-532fbce97db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355d0cf5-0e21-5469-ba66-35bad7819086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487668} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f83fb07-7636-5638-848b-073472f388c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e138229b-152f-5c0e-9e47-2d76a5b09b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-140130" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2861d1-b94e-575e-b228-b0ca92075f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269684} {:answer/id #uuid "388e326c-68ce-5a42-9e14-b5b28dfbbe60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269780} {:answer/id #uuid "84d25b97-36f7-5c25-bdf7-84fb45784022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270415} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a43c1c-9a33-5ff2-bec6-b39d1130f951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271142} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcbb417-958e-5ee5-832e-c203639f4cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5a43c1c-9a33-5ff2-bec6-b39d1130f951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271142} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9c0040-57fa-5c39-be56-9379977d3577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74386825-044a-58dc-81f5-45db80c2e6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140133" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]C=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $C:$ \n\n$$\nC={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $C$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c505c34a-6210-52dc-9732-f2260baedfa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402531} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8d32cf-4089-536a-8796-e014322aa487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402532} {:answer/id #uuid "7d507227-6d48-5cfc-b4f5-d6930a1040f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402533} {:answer/id #uuid "b30be1cb-84db-582c-9ee4-b5cbb0b9b4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402534} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0b88f1-c1f3-5ac7-ae96-efd7c30d9a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d507227-6d48-5cfc-b4f5-d6930a1040f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402533} :answer-field/id #uuid "a009d7d9-b93b-5ca2-863d-de3e1855e0a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18390ea4-94e2-564d-880f-13ae6fda894d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37272" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5f0e3f-05e4-5b32-8e42-33db9fecfeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392116} {:answer/id #uuid "afc41b45-077b-59fa-a6a2-e28ef564d7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392117} {:answer/id #uuid "e32ce8d9-f253-5c0c-9791-88ffd609ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392118} {:answer/id #uuid "d874ecd1-ed8a-514f-b148-263bd1e5349a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}97 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -97\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392119} {:answer/id #uuid "cd20040e-34e4-5440-afc8-b39be1193ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32ce8d9-f253-5c0c-9791-88ffd609ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392118} :answer-field/id #uuid "f73eb74f-7d8e-56f2-a6c1-4e42926c09f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7259ad7-f6a1-5f38-87c9-86ba246db7b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37597" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{97}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a52f5c-fb5a-567f-a05c-b4a3377da12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427851} {:answer/id #uuid "349ea5d5-1623-5b94-b22b-a5a6bd4fb201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427853} {:answer/id #uuid "65b8a476-fed9-5e7d-acb6-3aec390dd318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427857} {:answer/id #uuid "b20dbe0a-b3d3-556e-baa9-349554260ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427859} {:answer/id #uuid "f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3482122-1b23-51b6-bab9-0274990d6acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427860} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6614e3-dc36-51f8-9c16-862fd38745d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-37605" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1308c66e-451d-5de7-9708-faebf7ad18d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 24 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487608} {:answer/id #uuid "97e9238d-faff-5840-bb3c-d9b59bc98c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487852} {:answer/id #uuid "a6de871b-6bde-5f4b-84f9-b6d3cc4d1298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 12 \\\\ 30 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487879} {:answer/id #uuid "c689ce95-133d-55c0-9ab7-8f7fcb3be04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 12 \\\\ -30 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487945} {:answer/id #uuid "fb917ad6-d3d5-590e-b43c-bf793f4df7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -12 \\\\ -30 & -26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c689ce95-133d-55c0-9ab7-8f7fcb3be04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 12 \\\\ -30 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487945} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d99442-fd68-5c6b-a534-caad6d1d2c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "deae4223-84c9-5248-9dcf-afbc4aa0a08d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37623" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 24 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 12 \\\\ -30 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85014fe6-15e2-5564-bffa-ad90af465709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379805} {:answer/id #uuid "4c043ac2-85b1-59df-a20c-59478e1a4e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379806} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a42728-eef7-5a58-9f94-3c8639fc3ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & 24 \\\\ -20 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379807} {:answer/id #uuid "b5879874-53fc-5093-aa06-673de0a62779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -29 \\\\ 11 & -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379808} {:answer/id #uuid "65fa80e9-ff42-5610-849e-719df033e7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 & 3 \\\\ 32 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85014fe6-15e2-5564-bffa-ad90af465709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379805} :answer-field/id #uuid "de8ad135-a11b-5489-a19a-f9ef7b2d98a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "231a6159-8f35-5b7b-8f85-d4021873942f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37628" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{32},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{32}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{32} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[32]{3})^{32}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bb4f87-a553-5cdb-b80b-b631b4806733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321727} {:answer/id #uuid "6043b6d5-2b00-5716-9a69-b16e9d93b380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321902} {:answer/id #uuid "616a24c3-6314-553b-9c11-cdb0a248bd5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}70 & 32 \\\\ 32 & -52\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321910} {:answer/id #uuid "c780910b-6e0f-551e-a53d-4157c9a767da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322694} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a925df-9e30-5607-86c3-a1cf71d0b68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}70 & -13 \\\\ 17 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49bb4f87-a553-5cdb-b80b-b631b4806733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321727} :answer-field/id #uuid "eedd6128-abaf-5395-b478-267ab030e831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15dbc732-faa8-5570-a378-0344cccc0dac" :question/math-academy-id "q-37630" :question/problem "Find ${N}^{18}$ where\n\n$$\nN=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $N.$\n\n$$\nN={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $N=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${N}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{N}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{18} & =P{D}^{18}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{18}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[9]{2})^{18}\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 20 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2b6d9fc-27bd-59c7-939d-4c7647eba5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402704} {:answer/id #uuid "14f389ba-b888-572b-b782-3154d75be19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402705} {:answer/id #uuid "740563d7-e5ec-56c2-ad21-71bebf1921ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402706} {:answer/id #uuid "7e800d1f-0a48-5f64-afa7-82d01d638a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402707} {:answer/id #uuid "adfcc4ba-cd4b-5ff5-9647-ade01a07c322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "740563d7-e5ec-56c2-ad21-71bebf1921ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402706} :answer-field/id #uuid "7901325e-ab13-5590-bad7-aa03b8ccdd1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82160b99-37f5-52f2-85ae-e1d38b048f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-40138" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057aa010-a2f3-5746-b90b-2f137e1f8b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487576} {:answer/id #uuid "509646ef-2897-5ca4-89bc-3378e2e54d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 21 \\\\ 4 & -14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487727} {:answer/id #uuid "63e8d66b-1e0b-5f7d-b802-0676964edc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487766} {:answer/id #uuid "87b0027d-9cbb-5571-bb76-84b6d1b610e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}34 & 30 \\\\ -20 & -16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487825} {:answer/id #uuid "b80fd8b2-acfa-59aa-9fc1-c5b51d6b8e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057aa010-a2f3-5746-b90b-2f137e1f8b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81e4060-2609-58b6-b5bb-f912fcfd0e40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc5327fa-e153-5237-b670-7841684eaae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81214" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $H$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $H,$ then $H$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $H=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $H,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nH={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 21 \\\\ 4 & -14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271153a7-e008-5233-9e22-3f12deb489c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427852} {:answer/id #uuid "57dd64ab-9583-5552-b2ec-3be32f9e1309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}56 & -54 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427854} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3acd5e-d728-5511-ba7b-b5202ac2ddfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 9 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427855} {:answer/id #uuid "63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427856} {:answer/id #uuid "7154f91d-3460-5bf3-bea9-dc1e61a7a77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 51 & -49\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63ef4572-30b7-56ef-8f5a-ac03a89540d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427856} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a4b5644-3578-5ae2-81ef-1e895ed96291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "403fa166-7921-5239-b399-2bac8767c7a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86678" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c43ff2c-7024-5ab8-8bc2-22e31fa9e54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394726} {:answer/id #uuid "eaccd56a-34a7-52a0-b608-c985c9235d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394727} {:answer/id #uuid "a03b9d0e-11f1-566f-b9ba-fa0e60f97134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394728} {:answer/id #uuid "27c41af8-516f-5bde-9ba3-f1ed843addc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394729} {:answer/id #uuid "30c585ad-2172-536f-ba20-7aeb3cbaff02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c43ff2c-7024-5ab8-8bc2-22e31fa9e54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394726} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9688411-0118-5117-af08-62659c8482eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bac7350-7ccc-5944-ad6d-0daace45e031" :question/math-academy-id "q-86686" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{85},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{85} & =P{D}^{85}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{85}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{85} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{85}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27359bfd-be3c-5cf1-bd01-de39941cd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351924} {:answer/id #uuid "69b41b65-8c9f-57bf-a764-c4be901cae38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-60 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352504} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca878c6-3ac9-5a69-85fc-d735d7d1e6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -28 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352524} {:answer/id #uuid "c65ad6da-b0e6-5d01-9b41-a20ca0d5bf88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 60 \\\\ -18 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353289} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c25710-3be0-58ab-8eca-84880e70270f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27359bfd-be3c-5cf1-bd01-de39941cd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351924} :answer-field/id #uuid "11865484-c0f9-5b42-84f8-6f6395f0b142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c81d246-a69b-5179-85f7-88df79048f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86689" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{24},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{24}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} {:answer/id #uuid "28b0ed5c-9277-577c-b0f4-46d45ce08e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351944} {:answer/id #uuid "82f7010b-3ded-54da-922f-5d2a3139d0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352711} {:answer/id #uuid "b584defc-90ec-51d9-af5c-7473d8c554a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353119} {:answer/id #uuid "c68fd66d-ff23-5aca-a7e5-22e416ac2158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d0c1c4-db7a-5402-823c-10bcfe18c23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0ebddfd-78f1-5dda-8990-10394fcd7459" :question/math-academy-id "q-86693" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$"}]}
